# 1. Install Splunk Enterprise
1. Download Splunk Enterprise Free Trial from:
   https://www.splunk.com/en_us/download/splunk-enterprise.html
2. Install on Windows 10 using the 64-bit .msi installer.
3. Set an admin username and password when prompted.
4. After installation, open your browser and go to:
   http://localhost:8000

# 2. Project Folder Structure
├── Real-time threat detection with Splunk on Windows\
│
├── logs\
│   ├── apache_access.log           # Simulated Apache access logs
│   └── windows_events.csv          # Simulated Windows failed logins
│
├── detection_queries\
│   ├── brute_force_search.spl      # Detects repeated login attempts
│   ├── port_scan_search.spl        # Detects potential port scanning
│   └── windows_failed_logins.spl   # Detects excessive failed logins
│
├── log_stream_writer.py            # Continuously simulates new log data to apache_access.logs and windows_events.csv
├── threats_dashboard.png           # Screenshot of the live Splunk dashboard
├── README.md                       # Full project overview and explanation
└── setup_instructions.txt  

# 3. Configure Data Inputs in Splunk
1. In Splunk → Click **Settings > Add Data > Upload**.
2. Upload both files:
   - apache_access.log > set sourcetype = access_combined
   - windows_events.csv > set sourcetype = csv
3. Set **index = security** for both files.
4. Verify ingestion using: index=security

# 4. Run Real-Time Log Simulation
1. Open Command Prompt or PowerShell.
2. Navigate to project folder, and run: python log_stream_writer.py
3. This script will continuously append new log entries to apache_access.log and windows_events.csv.
4. Splunk will auto-detect and index new data in real-time.

# 5. Create and Save Detection Queries
1. Use the SPL (Search Processing Language) queries in **"detection_queries"** in Splunk Search.
2. Save each as a Report:
- Brute Force Detection
- Port Scan Detection
- Windows Failed Logins

# 6. Create the Dashboard
1. Go to **Search & Reporting -> Dashboards -> Create New Dashboard**.
2. Title: **Threat Dashboard**
3. Add Panels -> From Report:
   - Brute Force Detection
   - Detecting Port Scanning
   - Excessive Windows Failed Logins
4. Set auto-refresh to 30 seconds.
5. Save your layout.

# 7. Verify Dashboard
Ensure all panels update in real-time as new logs are simulated.
